  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIncreasing humidity and temperatures are expected through \nThursday with a warm front crossing just north of the area \nearlier in the day. Within this warmer and more humid airmass a \nfew scattered showers will be possible through tonight, but \nforcing is limited for any more organized activity. Showers will\nincrease in coverage starting early Thursday morning as both a \nweak sfc low and cold front approach and cross the region \nthrough the day. With PWat values increasing close to 2.00 \ninches during the day Thursday, heavier downpours will be \npossible with showers and thunderstorms that develop with \nincreasing instability. The greatest potential for thunderstorms\nis expected for the southwestern portions of the area, where \nsome slightly better instability is expected. Showers will end \nfrom west to east during the first half of the evening hours \nThursday as the cold front associated with the system tracks \neast across the area. Temperatures ahead of this cold front will\nwarm to the mid 70s, with some upper 70s to near 80 possible \nfor areas where there maybe more breaks in the showers and \nclouds. \n\nRainfall amounts on Thursday are expected to range from around a \nthird to half an inch, with some locally higher amounts possible \nwith thunderstorms that develop. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nBehind the passing cold front late Thursday, a cooler and drier\nairmass will settle into the region. High pressure will track \nsoutheast across Canada and into New England Friday through \nSaturday. This will result in mostly sunny/clear skies Friday \ninto early Saturday morning. With the cooler airmass associated \nwith the sfc high, temperatures for both Friday and Saturday \nwill only reach the mid 60s to low 70s. \n\nClouds will start to increase on Saturday afternoon ahead of the \nnext approaching system. More on that below. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nShowers will make a return to the region starting Saturday \nevening as a sfc low tracks out of the Tennessee Valley, \nnortheast to the mid-Atlantic coast through the day on Sunday. \nThis sfc low will pull moisture north as a cold front approaches\nfrom the northwest. There is still some uncertainty on the \ntrack of the sfc low and timing of the rain overall with the sfc\nhigh over the region earlier in the weekend providing some \nresistance to the incoming low. However, with that said the \npotential for widespread rainfall for most of Sunday is \npossible. Rain/showers will taper off Sunday evening. \n\nForecast guidance is not in the best agreement for the potential for \nshowers from Monday onward next week, with at least the chance for \nshowers each day from at least one model. This is just an indicator \nthat drier weather is less likely for next week at this far out.",
